{"slots": {"question": [{"claim": "The paper asks whether the friendship-formation patterns found in racially diverse schools, especially those tied to substance use, also hold in American Indian (AI)-majority reservation schools. Those schools are socially homogeneous, and the authors argue their peer dynamics are poorly understood.", "quotes": [{"text": "However, many schools located on American Indian (AI) reservations serve predominantly AI student bodies, creating a socially homogeneous environment that differs fundamentally from the diversified settings represented in prior literature.", "page": 2}, {"text": "It remains unclear whether these patterns hold in settings where the student population is predominantly AI and where cultural norms surrounding kinship, community, and identity may differently orient relational processes.", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "The specific aim is to examine what role substance use plays in forming friendship ties within AI-majority schools.", "quotes": [{"text": "This paper takes an initial step toward understanding these dynamics by investigating the role of substance use in the formation of friendship ties within AI‑majority schools.", "page": 2}]}], "central_claim": [{"claim": "In AI-majority reservation schools, friendships are driven mainly by structural mechanisms (reciprocity, transitivity) and by demographic homophily. Substance use and other risky behaviors do not organize these ties, which points to a distinct relational ecology.", "quotes": [{"text": "Results indicated that universal structural mechanisms (reciprocity, transitivity) and demographic homophily (gender, grade, sexual orientation) were primary drivers of friendship.", "page": 1}, {"text": "These findings reveal a distinct relational ecology within AI-majority reservation schools, where peer ties are organized around structural and demographic commonalities rather than risky behaviors.", "page": 1}]}, {"claim": "Substance use is a weaker organizing principle f