ubstantively meaningful association with friendship formation. This departs from patterns found in non-AI populations.", "quotes": [{"text": "It is important to note that depression likewise showed no substantively meaningful associations with friendship formation among AI adolescents, unlike the negative receiver effects and depression homophily commonly observed in non-AI populations [46]", "page": 14}, {"text": "AI identity homophily was consistently non-significant across all schools.", "page": 15}]}, {"claim": "Network processes differed by school context. The college-prep school (School C) alone showed a centralized popularity structure, and substance use homophily appeared only in the largest school (School B).", "quotes": [{"text": "Most notably, School C was the only setting where incoming ties were significantly centralized (negative GWIDeg)", "page": 15}, {"text": "Taken together, these patterns suggest that network processes in AI-majority schools are not uniform but are shaped by institutional context—school size, organizational type, and the strength of community embeddedness.", "page": 16}]}], "scope_and_limits": [{"claim": "The results come from only three schools on one reservation. They may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent only three schools on one reservation.", "page": 16}, {"text": "Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools, where Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "No comparable non-AI sample was available, so comparison with non-AI patterns is only indirect.", "quotes": [{"text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools would strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and contemporary data) was not feasible.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "Because the design is cross-sectional, the study cannot separate selection from influence. The results are associations, not causal effects, and cover only one developmental period at one time point.", "quotes": [{"text": "More importantly, cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends with similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].", "page": 16}, {"text": "not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as evidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.", "page": 17}, {"text": "we acknowledge that these data reflect only one developmental epoch of adolescents at one time point.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "Imputing substance use from peer perceptions may introduce bias, such as false consensus.", "quotes": [{"text": "First, adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus", "page": 7}]}, {"claim": "The demographic coefficients should not be read as causal effects. They are reported as descriptive benchmarks.", "quotes": [{"text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients as independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation", "page": 8}]}], "theoretical_positioning": [{"claim": "The authors place the study within adolescent peer-network research and revise its generalizations, which come from heterogeneous schools, by testing them in a culturally distinct AI-majority context.", "quotes": [{"text": "this research moves beyond generalizations derived from prior research and contributes new insights into the unique architecture of AI adolescent social worlds.", "page": 3}, {"text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation schools may alter these patterns.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "They combine network theory of endogenous structural mechanisms with a cultural view in which those mechanisms are shaped by community norms. They conclude that structural processes are universal but that their parameters depend on context.", "quotes": [{"text": "Critically, their prevalence and strength are not culturally neutral but are shaped by the social norms and relational expectations of a community [25].", "page": 3}, {"text": "Taken together, these findings suggest that while structural endogenous processes are universal, their specific parameters are calibrated by institutional context.", "page": 14}]}, {"claim": "They challenge the assumptions behind network-based prevention programs and call for culturally grounded theories and interventions.", "quotes": [{"text": "These findings challenge assumptions underpinning many network-based prevention programs and underscore the critical need for theories and interventions that are culturally and contextually grounded in the lived experiences of AI youth.", "page": 1}]}], "interlocutor_claim": [{"claim": "Prior studies in heterogeneous schools (e.g., Ali et al. [13]; Schaefer et al. [11]; Haas & Schaefer [22]) found that substance-using adolescents receive more nominations and that youth befriend peers with similar use. The paper disputes that these effects appear in AI-majority schools.", "quotes": [{"text": "Adolescents who use substances tend to receive more friendship nominations (positive receiver effects) [13], and homophily on substance use is well documented: youth are more likely to befriend peers with similar consumption patterns [11,22].", "page": 4}, {"text": "By contrast, in our AI-majority sample, substance use showed no consistent sender, receiver, or homophily effects across schools.", "page": 14}]}, {"claim": "Rees, Freng, and Winfree used Add Health and found that AI adolescents in heterogeneous schools had fewer reciprocated friendships than White peers. The authors build on this work while criticizing it as descriptive and lacking models of endogenous processes.", "quotes": [{"text": "One study that did focus specifically on AI youth network characteristics is Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17], who used Add Health to describe AI networks in heterogeneous school contexts, finding that AI adolescents had fewer reciprocated friendships than White peers.", "page": 2}, {"text": "However, their analysis was primarily descriptive and did not model the endogenous network processes examined here.", "page": 2}]}, {"claim": "The prevailing approach to network interventions for substance use prevention assumes that friendships are partly founded on substance use. The authors dispute that this assumption holds for AI youth.", "quotes": [{"text": "The prevalent approach to adolescent social network interventions for substance use prevention often hinges on the presumption that friendship ties are, to some extent, founded on substance use behaviors.", "page": 16}]}, {"claim": "Schaefer, Kornienko, and Fox showed that depression homophily can arise from withdrawal by depressed adolescents rather than from avoidance by peers. The authors build on this by testing which depression mechanisms operate in AI-majority schools.", "quotes": [{"text": "For example, Schaefer, Kornienko, and Fox (2011) show that depression homophily can emerge from withdrawal by depressed egos rather than from avoidance by alters [31].", "page": 4}, {"text": "We therefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily— operate in AI-majority school contexts.", "page": 4}]}, {"claim": "Prior research (McFarland et al. [1]) holds that homophily on deviant behaviors grows as school size increases. The authors build on this to explain why substance use homophily appeared only in the largest school.", "quotes": [{"text": "This finding aligns with research suggesting that homophily on deviant behaviors becomes more salient as school